#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097469} :activity/id #uuid "f64fb60f-19cd-5d89-bcbf-86fc646151c5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048882 :topic/id #uuid "c968c8f6-9b7b-5304-a2de-0e2f2c824aa9" :topic/math-academy-id 1101} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097829 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a vector\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\textbf{a}\n$$\n\nWe denote its **magnitude**, also known as its *modulus* or *length*, as $\\mid \\overrightarrow{AB} \\mid$ or $\\mid \\textbf{a} \\mid$.\n\nFor example, the vector below has a magnitude of $8$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\overrightarrow{AB} &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/71105c9d5920ec71e61fe1176021024188a86bd8023671b79e0ce89082994299.png)\n\nNow, let's consider the following situation. Suppose that the vector $\\textbf{a}$ is directed vertically upwards and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\textbf{a} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile $\\textbf{b}$ is directed horizontally to the right and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\textbf{b} &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow can we find $\\mid \\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b} \\mid$?\n\nFirst, we use the triangle law of addition to find the resultant $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/ff/ffd155d3b72015a72d878fc8089381a5bcf5a97f698f85945642189ea7368a3f.png)\n\nNotice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 6^{2} + 8^{2} \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b} &= \\sqrt{100}=10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: In the above calculation we wrote\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & ^2 = & \\mathbf{a} & ^2+\\left.\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{b}\\right|^2\n$$\n\n. This works only when the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are at right angles, and the relationship is not true in general. We'll see how to deal with non-right-angled vectors soon." :tutorial/id #uuid "2584bcd4-11ba-581a-a0b2-10a7b2854dfc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2902 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "15159922-befb-577b-93fb-cc8cd2981961" :step/math-academy-id 15687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097471}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097471 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096643 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096440 :question/id #uuid "8cc1617d-ba3f-5ae3-aca0-90354ffbd62f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4273"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48579a8d-61f0-50c1-81f6-7b087b3f936c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049603}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097008}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Pythagorean Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "ec39e834-5cb5-545e-b5d3-96ee21673966" :step/math-academy-id 15689 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096645 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096442 :question/id #uuid "f0c94cce-790d-50b0-bd9b-13b64867848b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4275"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c145c97c-c313-597f-b84e-8adb3bdf9c14" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050710}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097017}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Law of Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "f595e55d-b2aa-50bf-8d57-1c6eaf004504" :step/math-academy-id 15692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097839 :tutorial/content "When the triangle law for addition does *not* give a right triangle, we *cannot* use the Pythagorean theorem to compute the magnitude of the sum.\n\nFor example, using the triangle law to draw the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ for the vectors below, we do not get a right triangle. Instead, we get a triangle with an obtuse angle $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/96/967966ec9d8ac0b7358623645de0e7931ef4d6cc87bd919d1f0cf04f7ca6ca28.png)\n\nWe do not obtain a right triangle here, so we cannot use the Pythagorean theorem. However, we can use the law of cosines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} + & b & ^{2} - 2 & a\\end{vmatrix} \\mid b \\mid \\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given any two vectors that form a triangle using the triangle addition law, we can always use the law of cosines to compute the magnitude of the sum." :tutorial/id #uuid "1a74f53c-fedc-56dc-80cb-691a8a057007" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2962 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Using the Law of Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "ff54536c-3852-5ce2-8d8f-43b7291868cb" :step/math-academy-id 16029 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096690 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096485 :question/id #uuid "ba160db3-7799-5a25-8c50-5147100f7475" :question/math-academy-id "e-5013"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0146b8-421b-5d63-a933-3de9bcaa7ab2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097172}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Magnitude of a Vector From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "2412a5fc-6f07-508c-bfdf-341b7a606b74" :step/math-academy-id 18402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097491}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097805 :tutorial/content "![](images/0a/0ac79071675ca17dbb25708f8131a0f1706c29ffe69c750969112fd6a097d1f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{b} &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow can we find $\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} \\mid$?\n\nFirst, we use the triangle law of addition to find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/dd/dd9b407a04394d9648db4671788791a8cf615b7755c1efead6f637fe26a40562.png)\n\nWe do not obtain a right triangle here, so we cannot use the Pythagorean theorem. But we can use the law of cosines, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} + & b & ^{2} - 2 & a\\end{vmatrix} \\mid b \\mid \\cos (120^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 4^{2} + 5^{2} - 2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= 16 + 25 + 20 \\\\\n&= 61\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{61}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9bdfe6fa-0c60-5f19-a5e8-3d7a07872026" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13222 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "1e20290a-9c6e-5b7f-9e29-a74378de3216" :step/math-academy-id 457894 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097474}}] :activity/title "The Magnitude of a Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096881}]}
